1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,387
ERP and EIRP answer a deceptively simple question: how much power is that

2
00:00:06,387 --> 00:00:08,250
transmitter really putting out?

3
00:00:08,250 --> 00:00:13,117
Not what the amplifier makes — what actually leaves the antenna, in its strongest

4
00:00:13,117 --> 00:00:13,718
direction.

5
00:00:13,718 --> 00:00:18,645
Both are effective radiated power measures that fold the transmitter's output, the

6
00:00:18,645 --> 00:00:22,431
feedline's losses, and the antenna's gain into a single number.

7
00:00:22,431 --> 00:00:26,698
The only difference between them is the ruler they're measured against.

8
00:00:27,148 --> 00:00:28,601
Start with a flashlight.

9
00:00:28,601 --> 00:00:33,629
The bulb makes the same light either way, but the reflector squeezes it into a beam

10
00:00:33,629 --> 00:00:37,082
— and along that beam, it looks like a far brighter bulb.

11
00:00:37,082 --> 00:00:39,868
Antenna gain works exactly like the reflector.

12
00:00:39,868 --> 00:00:44,895
A passive antenna radiates no more total power than it's fed; gain is directivity —

13
00:00:44,895 --> 00:00:47,379
energy concentrated into a narrower beam.

14
00:00:47,379 --> 00:00:52,164
Along the main lobe the field is stronger than an omnidirectional antenna would

15
00:00:52,164 --> 00:00:54,648
produce, and off to the side it's weaker.

16
00:00:54,648 --> 00:00:59,433
That's why a high-power directional link can be nearly invisible from the wrong

17
00:00:59,433 --> 00:00:59,796
angle.

18
00:01:00,246 --> 00:01:03,308
The bookkeeping is a short chain of decibels.

19
00:01:03,308 --> 00:01:07,526
Take the transmitter's output — say plus forty dBm, ten watts.

20
00:01:07,526 --> 00:01:12,289
Subtract the feedline and connector losses — two dB lost up the tower.

21
00:01:12,289 --> 00:01:14,602
Add the antenna's gain — nine dBi.

22
00:01:14,602 --> 00:01:18,072
Forty minus two plus nine: forty-seven dBm of EIRP.

23
00:01:18,072 --> 00:01:23,583
That's the power an ideal antenna radiating equally in every direction would need

24
00:01:23,583 --> 00:01:28,822
to match this system's peak — even though the amplifier only ever made forty.

25
00:01:29,272 --> 00:01:31,522
So what separates the two names?

26
00:01:31,522 --> 00:01:33,420
Just the reference antenna.

27
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:39,045
EIRP compares against an ideal isotropic radiator — a mathematical point source,

28
00:01:39,045 --> 00:01:40,733
equal in all directions.

29
00:01:40,733 --> 00:01:45,515
ERP compares against a half-wave dipole — a real, buildable antenna.

30
00:01:45,515 --> 00:01:51,280
A lossless dipole already has 2.15 dB of gain over isotropic, so the conversion is

31
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:54,374
fixed forever: EIRP equals ERP plus 2.15 dB.

32
00:01:54,374 --> 00:01:59,648
Broadcasters and land-mobile regulators traditionally quote ERP; satellite,

33
00:01:59,648 --> 00:02:05,062
microwave, and radar engineers quote EIRP, because the isotropic reference is

34
00:02:05,062 --> 00:02:05,976
cleaner math.

35
00:02:06,426 --> 00:02:11,243
And here's why regulators cap these numbers instead of raw transmitter power:

36
00:02:11,243 --> 00:02:15,058
interference and exposure depend on what's actually radiated.

37
00:02:15,058 --> 00:02:19,874
A Wi-Fi rule of thirty-six dBm EIRP lets you trade a bigger antenna against a

38
00:02:19,874 --> 00:02:23,877
smaller amplifier, so long as the product stays under the limit.

39
00:02:23,877 --> 00:02:28,944
A broadcast license of fifty kilowatts ERP fixes the station's effective reach no

40
00:02:28,944 --> 00:02:33,135
matter how that power splits between the amplifier and the antenna.

41
00:02:33,135 --> 00:02:35,449
The cap is on the field, not the box.

42
00:02:35,899 --> 00:02:40,934
As a listener, you'll never radiate an ERP of your own — but every signal you hear

43
00:02:40,934 --> 00:02:42,285
was launched with one.

44
00:02:42,285 --> 00:02:47,320
A trunking control channel, a broadcast tower, an aircraft transponder: each one's

45
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:52,355
EIRP sets how strong it arrives at your antenna after the path takes its toll, and

46
00:02:52,355 --> 00:02:55,302
hence whether it clears your noise floor at all.

47
00:02:55,302 --> 00:03:00,337
Better still, for licensed land-mobile and broadcast transmitters the ERP is often

48
00:03:00,337 --> 00:03:05,434
on public record — so you can look up how loudly a site shouts before you ever tune

49
00:03:05,434 --> 00:03:05,802
to it.

50
00:03:06,252 --> 00:03:09,258
That makes ERP a planning tool for GopherTrunk.

51
00:03:09,258 --> 00:03:14,565
If a site's published power and its distance imply a weak arriving signal, the dBFS

52
00:03:14,565 --> 00:03:19,552
level and SNR readout in GopherTrunk's interface will confirm it — and the fix

53
00:03:19,552 --> 00:03:24,668
lives on your side of the link: a better receive antenna, a low-noise amplifier,

54
00:03:24,668 --> 00:03:26,074
some gain of your own.

55
00:03:26,074 --> 00:03:29,527
The decoder can only work with what survives the trip.

56
00:03:29,977 --> 00:03:35,718
So: ERP and EIRP fold transmitter power, cable loss, and antenna gain into one

57
00:03:35,718 --> 00:03:41,311
radiated-power figure; they differ only by reference antenna — dipole versus

58
00:03:41,311 --> 00:03:47,420
isotropic, exactly 2.15 dB apart; and antenna gain is a reflector, not an amplifier

59
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:49,775
— concentration, never creation.

60
00:03:49,775 --> 00:03:51,762
Full write-up linked below.

